Subject: A compiled activation that finishes in the interpreter on a frame that is not light-safe is stepped

What this does
  - `regvm_aot_run_to_end` runs the threaded interpreter on the frame only when the function's code is light-safe (`aot_light` or `regvm_light`). Otherwise it steps the frame with `regvm_step`, one instruction at a time, on a copy of the frame's header prefix that has the record-only tail the steps read (`argv`, `new_target`, `ctx`), and returns the value (or the exception, with its backtrace built at the throw).

Why we need it
  The frames that compiled code builds for itself (the exit frames of the native entries, the frames of inlined levels) are light frames: no JSStackFrame. The monolith's handlers for instructions the threaded chain does not run (closure variable access, arguments) read `f->sf->cur_func`, which is NULL on such a frame. The generated generic body avoided this by stepping those instructions through the step twins, which read `f->func`.

Failure
  Not reachable from the code the generator emitted until now. With the generic bodies replaced by a continuation in the interpreter (QJS_AOT_NOGENERIC), an exit in an inlined callee that reads a closure variable ran `regvm_exec_one` on a light frame: EXC_BAD_ACCESS at `sf->cur_func`. vcase f31-inline-closure-cold-copy.

Soundness
  The stepped path executes the same instruction handlers the generic body called, on the same frame, with the same exception conventions. The light-safe path is unchanged.
--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -69806,0 +69807,4 @@
+    JSFunctionBytecode *b = f->b;
+    JSRegRun tmp;
+    uint8_t *ip;
+
@@ -69808,2 +69812,38 @@
-    regvm_t_dispatch(f->b->regvm_code + pc, fr, f, f->b->realm);
-    return f->ret;
+    if (likely(b->aot_light || b->regvm_light)) {
+        regvm_t_dispatch(b->regvm_code + pc, fr, f, b->realm);
+        return f->ret;
+    }
+    /* The code is not light-safe (closure variables, arguments, control records, an instruction only the monolith runs): the
+       monolith's handlers read the frame's JSStackFrame, which a light frame does not have. Step it, as the generated
+       generic body does for the instructions it does not lower: each step runs on the frame and needs no JSStackFrame. */
+    /* The steps read the record-only tail of the frame for the arguments (`rf->argv`) and `new.target`: a light frame ends after the
+       header prefix. Step on a copy of the prefix that has a tail (the registers, the variable area and the arguments stay where
+       they are). */
+    memset(&tmp, 0, sizeof(tmp));
+    memcpy(&tmp, f, REGVM_LIGHT_PREFIX_BYTES);
+    tmp.ctx = b->realm;
+    tmp.caller_ctx = ctx;
+    tmp.new_target = JS_UNDEFINED;
+    tmp.argv = f->arg_buf;
+    tmp.deopt_pc = QJS_AOT_NO_DEOPT;
+    ctx->rt->regvm_cur = &tmp;
+    ip = b->regvm_code + pc;
+    for (;;) {
+        uint8_t *nip;
+
+        tmp.cap_ip = ip;
+        nip = regvm_step(ip, fr, &tmp, b->realm);
+        if (nip == NULL) {
+            if (needs_backtrace(ctx->rt->current_exception))
+                build_backtrace(ctx, ctx->rt->current_exception, JS_UNDEFINED, NULL, 0, 0, 0);
+            ctx->rt->regvm_cur = f;
+            return JS_EXCEPTION;
+        }
+        if (nip == REGVM_COLD_RET) {
+            ctx->rt->regvm_cur = f;
+            if (unlikely(JS_IsException(tmp.ret)) && needs_backtrace(ctx->rt->current_exception))
+                build_backtrace(ctx, ctx->rt->current_exception, JS_UNDEFINED, NULL, 0, 0, 0);
+            return tmp.ret;
+        }
+        ip = nip;
+    }
